# 0001. Record architecture decisions as .adr files

- Status: Accepted
- Date: 2026-11-11

Decisions with long-term consequences for this config are recorded as numbered `.adr` files
in `docs/adr/`, so the why is in one place instead of scattered through history.

## Technical details
- **Problem**: the config's decisions lived in commit messages, README "review" sections and
  code comments; a future reader (or Claude) had to dig through history to learn why things
  are the way they are.
- **Files**: `docs/adr/NNNN-short-title.adr`: sequential four-digit numbers, never reused;
  Markdown content with the `.adr` extension so they are told apart from other docs.
  `docs/adr/README.md` is the index (number, title, status).
- **Format**: the `adr-manager` template: title, `- Status:` and `- Date:` lines, a one-line
  purpose, `## Technical details`, `## Technologies and alternatives`. Statuses: Proposed,
  Accepted, Rejected, Deprecated, Superseded (with links both ways).
- **History**: 0002-0012 were recorded retroactively from the repo's history, README
  "Earlier review" / "Design pass" and the commits that made each change.
- **Commits**: every subject carries the ADR it falls under (`[ADR-NNNN]`, minor changes
  `[ADR-NNNN.N]`, `commit-style`); `CLAUDE.md` says to read the ADR before changing a
  recorded decision.
- **Consequences**: one place, one format, with a status showing whether a decision still
  holds. `.adr` needs editor support ([0012](0012-adr-files-in-the-editor.adr)).

## Technologies and alternatives
- Alternatives: decisions in README sections: mixed with usage docs, no status or
  supersession; `.md` extension: works everywhere, but ADRs can't be told apart.
